course: dictionary
lesson: 5000
title: Indulgent: too kind, or a treat
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Indulgent describes someone who lets you have what you want, even when it is not good for you. A grown-up who is indulgent is kind but not strict. Look at the clues around the word: what does the person let happen?
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat is often indulgent to Toad and Mole, and Badger is less so. Badger keeps firmer rules, so the Rat is the softer friend who lets them have their way. The context tells you who is soft and who is firm.
explain: The word can also mean a treat. An indulgent chocolate cake is a cake you eat to enjoy yourself. Test each meaning by putting it into the sentence and asking whether it still makes sense. The Rat teases Mole with «Self-indulgent beggar you seem to be, Mole … Deny yourself nothing», and it is said as a friendly joke.
fact: Indulgent is an adjective: indulgent, more indulgent, most indulgent.
fact: «Indulgent» can mean letting someone have what they want, even if it is not good for them.
fact: «Indulgent» can also mean treating yourself to something pleasant, such as an indulgent chocolate cake.
fact: «Self-indulgent» means doing too much of what you like.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat is often indulgent to Toad and Mole, and Badger is less so.
exercise: «Indulgent parents let their children stay up late and eat whatever they like.» What does indulgent mean here? | Angry and loud | Strict and firm about rules | Tired and sleepy | *Too kind and not strict, letting people have what they want
exercise: Which word means doing too much of what you like? | silent | *self-indulgent | gentle | strict
exercise: «She ate an indulgent chocolate cake after her test.» What does indulgent mean here? | *Something pleasant that you treat yourself to | Something very old and dusty | A cake that is too hard to eat | Something made in a hurry
test: In The Wind in the Willows, which character is less indulgent than the Rat? | Mole | Toad | *Badger
test: Which sentence shows someone being indulgent? | *«Go on, have another biscuit, it's fine» | «Tidy your room before you go out» | «Put your shoes on now» | «No sweets until after dinner, and that is final»
exam: Which meaning fits in «She bought herself an indulgent ice cream after a hard day»? | Too cold to eat | *Treating herself to something pleasant | Given away for free to strangers | Very, very old
